=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: ispb
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** FRR ospfd on ispb — `passive-interface eth0` in the `router ospf` block of ispb's frr.conf, intentionally blocking OSPF hello transmission on the interface that faces R1.

**Mechanism:** ispb is fully configured as an OSPF participant on eth0 (`ip ospf area 0`, `redistribute connected`, `default-information originate`) and shares the 203.0.113.4/30 segment with R1's eth3 (203.0.113.6/30). In normal operation ispb should form an OSPF adjacency with R1 on that link, advertising its connected ISP prefixes (216.239.35.4/32 loopback, 192.0.2.0/30 transit to ispa) and originating a second default route. With `passive-interface eth0` injected, ispb sends no hellos; no adjacency ever forms. R1's OSPF neighbor table therefore shows only csw1 and csw2 — ispb is the missing (lost) third neighbor. R1 falls back to a single static kernel default via ispa (203.0.113.1, eth2). With only one ISP uplink and no redundancy, any ispa-side congestion or transient loss causes new TCP connections to 203.0.113.x to time out before the kernel eventually retransmits or the client retries; established sessions on the live ispa path survive uninterrupted.

**Localization:** Device ispb, interface eth0, `router ospf` stanza — the comment `! Passive Interface to R1 [Source 93]` in frr.conf confirms the injected fault.

**Root cause:** `passive-interface eth0` on ispb prevents the intended R1↔ispb OSPF adjacency, eliminating redundant internet routing via the ispb ISP path.

**Fix:** Remove `passive-interface eth0` from ispb's `router ospf` block and add `network 203.0.113.4/30 area 0` (or `ip ospf area 0`) to R1's eth3 interface so both sides participate in OSPF on the CD_ISPB_R1 segment, restoring the adjacency and dual-ISP default routing.
=== END ===